<p>
This feels like being at a bar, where one friend wants to pick a fight with someone, another friend -- a sadistic one -- eggs him on, and a third quietly sitting between them, and being ignored, throws the first punch mostly to get attention. <br />
Well here goes: Why I am not a fan of Dan Barry's work. <br />
He comes off as disingenuous. <br />
He relies too much on technique -- vocabulary, style, play on words -- to hide what is essentially a lack of feeling. And this comes off as dishonest. <br />
For me, the best writing is that in which the writers reveal something about themselves: what their fears are, their likes, their embarassments. It's about taking risks, putting YOURSELF out there. I'm not talking first-person. In the aggregate, the choice of details, observations, and themes in a story tell readers how much the writer felt during the writing. <br />
And you can't hide this with style. It's not good enough just to have a heart. You also have to be willing to have that heart stomped on, spit on and then vindicated to be a good writer.   <br />
Barry's stories mostly say, he's a very good but safe technician. He does not take many personal risks with stories.<br />
That said. I liked this piece Barry did on the homeless men. It provided a model for daily news writing, and it came through that he cared about the men, even if it was from an arms-length.  
</p>
