<p>
Sully, <br />
<br />
All right. I'll swing back (and are you calling Ben a sadist?)<br />
I see what you're saying. On the spectrum of great narrative writers, Barry is towards the lower end of those who put a lot of themselves into their stories. He does keep his distance, compared to, say, Norman Mailer. <br />
Personally I have no problem with that. <br />
Part of it's a limitation of the form - he's writing for the New York Times (how many NYT pieces can you think of where someone put their heart out to be stomped on?) And part of it's the realization, I think, that the story's not about him, and shouldn't be.<br />
He's not writing about "Big Events," that effect us all, like Mailer. He's not writing about his own life, like, say, Charlie LeDuff in that fatherhood piece. Or even about things he's experienced personally, like Ernie Pyle in World War Two. He's just writing about everyday people, trying to live their lives.<br />
And, really, he's probably quite different from most of those people, being a highly-successful writer from the lofty New York Times and all. What personal risks in telling their stories is he supposed to take? What, really, would his personal experience bring to the table of their story? Better, in this case anyway, to stay at a distance, to be a conduit, to let the story speak for itself. It's more dignified that way. That's my opinion, at least.<br />
By the way, if you want a peek at the man's soul, read his book, Pull Me Up. It's certainly there.
</p>
